# =============================================================================
# 20-mesh.nft — WireGuard mesh trust boundary
# =============================================================================
# This is the CORE of the policy.  Traffic is split into two worlds:
#
#   World A — arrived through the WireGuard tunnel (wg0):
#             These packets come from authenticated peers.  WireGuard's
#             cryptographic handshake guarantees the sender holds the
#             private key that matches a pre-authorised public key on
#             the server.  We validate the source IP and jump to the
#             "mesh_input" sub-chain where per-service rules live.
#
#   World B — everything else (eth0, the public internet):
#             Unconditionally dropped (logged first by 70-logging.nft).
#
# Why trust wg0 at the interface level and not just the source IP?
#   Source IPs can be spoofed on the public internet.
#   A packet exiting wg0 has ALREADY been decrypted with the session key —
#   it cannot have been forged by a non-peer.  Interface-level trust is
#   therefore cryptographically sound for WireGuard.
#
# BUG FIX (1A) — anti-spoof check order:
#   The spoof check MUST come BEFORE the unconditional jump to mesh_input.
#   If the jump fires first, control never returns to the input chain for
#   wg0 packets — the spoof rule becomes unreachable dead code.
#   Rule order: spoof-drop -> break-glass (non-wg0 only) -> jump ->
#   (mesh_input handles everything else)
#
# BUG FIX (v4-02) — break-glass no longer jumps the anti-spoof queue:
#   v3 placed the ADMIN_ALLOWLIST break-glass SSH rules BEFORE the spoof-drop
#   pairs and with no interface qualifier, so a spoofed/misrouted wg0 packet
#   matching ADMIN_ALLOWLIST + dport 22 was accepted before the anti-spoof
#   check ever ran.  Break-glass now sits AFTER both spoof-drop pairs and is
#   qualified "iifname != wg0" — it only ever answers on the public
#   interface, exactly as intended ("fires when WireGuard is down").
#
# Topology assumed:
#   WireGuard interface : wg0
#   VPN server FQDN     : vpn.yourdomain.com
#   Mesh CIDR (IPv4)    : 10.10.0.0/24
#   Server's mesh IP    : 10.10.0.1
#   Peer range          : 10.10.0.2 – 10.10.0.254
#
# To use a different interface name (e.g. wg1, tailscale0, utun*):
#   Change every "wg0" reference in this file AND in 10-antiscan.nft.
#
# BUG FIX (v3-03) — mesh ICMP lived in dead code:
#   mesh_input always terminates (accept, or the final drop), so wg0 traffic
#   NEVER reached the ICMP rules in 60-icmp.nft — mesh peers could not ping
#   the server at all.  ICMP for the mesh is now handled HERE, inside
#   mesh_input, before the services jump.  60-icmp.nft covers the public
#   interface only.
# =============================================================================

table inet filter {
    chain input {

        # --- Anti-spoof: drop wg0 packets with non-mesh source IP FIRST ---
        # BUG FIX (1A): This must precede the unconditional jump below.
        # If a decrypted packet exits wg0 but its source IP is outside the
        # mesh CIDR, something is misconfigured (wrong AllowedIPs on the
        # WireGuard server, or a tampered peer config).
        # This should never fire in normal operation — if it does, investigate.
        iifname "wg0" ip saddr != @MESH_PEERS \
            limit rate 5/second burst 10 packets \
            log prefix "XNFT-MESH-SPOOF: " level warn \
            comment "mesh: log spoofed IPv4 in tunnel (rate-limited)"
        iifname "wg0" ip saddr != @MESH_PEERS \
            counter drop comment "mesh: spoofed source IP inside wg0 tunnel"

        # IPv6 anti-spoof.  ACTIVE even with MESH_PEERS6 empty (BUG v3-06):
        # v2 left this commented out, so IPv6 packets inside the tunnel
        # bypassed source validation and fell to the mesh_input catch-all —
        # same verdict (drop) but unvalidated and mislabelled in logs.  With
        # an empty set the rule simply drops ALL IPv6 in the tunnel, which is
        # the correct deny-by-default for an IPv4-only mesh.
        iifname "wg0" ip6 saddr != @MESH_PEERS6 \
            limit rate 5/second burst 10 packets \
            log prefix "XNFT-MESH-SPOOF6: " level warn \
            comment "mesh: log spoofed IPv6 in tunnel (rate-limited)"
        iifname "wg0" ip6 saddr != @MESH_PEERS6 \
            counter drop comment "mesh: spoofed IPv6 source inside wg0 tunnel"

        # --- Break-glass emergency SSH (ADMIN_ALLOWLIST, post-spoof-drop) ---
        # This MUST be here in the input chain — not the services chain,
        # which is only reached via wg0 — because it needs to fire when
        # WireGuard is DOWN.  Populated only if ADMIN_ALLOWLIST has entries
        # (00-tables.nft).
        #
        # BUG FIX (v4-02) — break-glass bypassed the mesh anti-spoof check:
        #   v3 placed these three rules BEFORE the spoof-drop pairs above and
        #   with no "iifname" qualifier.  A packet arriving ON wg0 itself with
        #   a source address inside @ADMIN_ALLOWLIST and dport 22 was accepted
        #   by the old rule 1 before the anti-spoof check ever ran — exactly
        #   the "WireGuard AllowedIPs misconfigured more broadly than the mesh
        #   CIDR" case BUG 1A exists to catch, defeated for the one port an
        #   attacker most wants.  It also let such a packet skip the per-source
        #   SSH meter in 40-services.nft entirely (different chain, different
        #   meter).  Fix: qualify to the non-mesh path and move the three
        #   rules below both spoof-drop pairs, so a wg0 packet always hits the
        #   spoof check first regardless of its source address.
        #
        # BUG FIX (v4-03) — global (not per-source) rate limit:
        #   "limit rate 10/minute" is a single shared token bucket despite the
        #   comment claiming "per source" — the same bug class already fixed
        #   as BUG 3A (50-vpn-endpoint.nft) and BUG v3-09 (40-services.nft) but
        #   missed here.  Worse than those two: the accept only needs a SYN to
        #   consume a token, and TCP source addresses are spoofable for a SYN.
        #   The break-glass IP is not a secret (it is in git history, in the
        #   set dump, in "nft list ruleset" output) — an attacker sending 1 pps
        #   of spoofed SYNs "from" that address permanently drains the shared
        #   bucket and welds the emergency door shut, on the one path you use
        #   when WireGuard is already down.  Fix: give each source its own
        #   bucket via a meter, matching the ssh_rl pattern in 40-services.nft.
        iifname != "wg0" ip saddr @ADMIN_ALLOWLIST \
            tcp dport 22 \
            ct state new \
            meter breakglass_rl size 1024 { ip saddr timeout 60s limit rate over 10/minute } \
            jump breakglass_ratelimit_drop comment "break-glass: per-source rate-limit (IPv4)"
        iifname != "wg0" ip saddr @ADMIN_ALLOWLIST \
            tcp dport 22 \
            ct state new \
            accept comment "break-glass: emergency SSH (ADMIN_ALLOWLIST, public iface)"

        # --- WireGuard tunnel: jump to per-service allowlist ---
        # Only reached after the spoof check above passes.
        # We use "jump" (not "goto") so if mesh_input returns without a
        # verdict, the packet falls through to 70-logging.nft's catch-all.
        iifname "wg0" jump mesh_input comment "mesh: enter service allowlist"
    }

    chain mesh_input {
        # BUG FIX (1B / 2A): conntrack checks MUST run inside mesh_input.
        # Without these, a compromised mesh peer sending invalid-state packets
        # bypasses the conntrack gate in 30-established.nft entirely, because
        # that chain is evaluated AFTER this jump and only for packets that
        # return from mesh_input — but mesh_input always terminates.

        # Drop invalid conntrack state from mesh peers.
        #
        # BUG FIX (v4-01): rate-limit the log, split from the drop — a
        # compromised or misbehaving peer inside the mesh should not be able
        # to flood the log any more than a public-interface attacker can
        # (see 30-established.nft and 10-antiscan.nft for the same pattern).
        ct state invalid \
            limit rate 10/second burst 20 packets \
            log prefix "XNFT-MESH-INVALID: " level warn \
            comment "mesh: log conntrack invalid state (rate-limited)"
        ct state invalid \
            counter drop comment "mesh: conntrack invalid state"

        # Fast-path for established/related connections (e.g. long-lived SSH
        # sessions, streaming responses).  Must come after invalid-drop.
        ct state { established, related } \
            accept comment "mesh: conntrack established/related fast-path"

        # --- Mesh ICMP (BUG FIX v3-03: moved here from 60-icmp.nft) ---
        # Ping between authenticated peers is an operational necessity
        # (reachability checks, MTU debugging).  Rate-limited to keep a
        # compromised peer from using echo floods as a DoS vector.
        ip saddr @MESH_PEERS \
            icmp type echo-request \
            limit rate 10/second burst 20 packets \
            accept comment "mesh: ping from peers (rate-limited)"

        ip6 saddr @MESH_PEERS6 \
            icmpv6 type echo-request \
            limit rate 10/second burst 20 packets \
            accept comment "mesh: IPv6 ping from peers (rate-limited)"

        # ICMP errors (dest-unreachable, time-exceeded, PMTUD) for existing
        # flows are already covered by the established/related fast-path
        # above — conntrack marks ICMP errors for known flows as "related".

        # Source-address validation: ensure the decrypted packet's source IP
        # belongs to a known mesh peer.  This catches the case where WireGuard
        # AllowedIPs is misconfigured more broadly than the mesh CIDR.
        ip saddr @MESH_PEERS \
            jump services comment "mesh: known IPv4 peer → services"

        # IPv6 mesh peers — no-op while MESH_PEERS6 is empty, active the
        # moment you populate the set (00-tables.nft).
        ip6 saddr @MESH_PEERS6 \
            jump services comment "mesh: known IPv6 peer → services"

        # A known peer whose traffic matched nothing in the services chain
        # lands here (the spoof check upstream already removed unknown
        # sources).  Renamed from XNFT-MESH-UNKNOWN (v2) which mislabelled
        # every denied-port packet as an "unknown source" (BUG v3-07).
        limit rate 5/second burst 10 packets \
            log prefix "XNFT-MESH-DENY: " level info \
            comment "mesh: log peer traffic to non-allowlisted port (rate-limited)"
        counter drop comment "mesh: peer port not in services allowlist, drop"
    }

    # Rate-limited log + unconditional drop for break-glass SSH offenders.
    # Separate chain so the log's own rate limit can never gate the drop
    # (see 10-antiscan.nft header for the pattern rationale) — same shape as
    # ssh_ratelimit_drop in 40-services.nft and wg_ratelimit_drop in
    # 50-vpn-endpoint.nft.  Per-source meter (BUG FIX v4-03) means only the
    # offending source lands here; every other admin IP is unaffected.
    chain breakglass_ratelimit_drop {
        limit rate 6/minute burst 10 packets \
            log prefix "XNFT-BREAKGLASS-RATELIMIT: " level warn \
            comment "break-glass: log rate-limit excess (rate-limited)"
        counter drop comment "break-glass: rate-limit exceeded"
    }
}
